/// @module std::core::hooks
/// The `before`-hook decision protocol (ADR-009 E4).
///
/// A `before` hook installed by an annotation may return a `HookDecision<Sig>`
/// to either CONTINUE to the target with a (possibly-rebound) argument pack, or
/// SHORT-CIRCUIT with a result — without ever running the target. After-hooks
/// STILL run on a short-circuit (the wrapping/onion semantic).
///
/// ## Surface only — the compiler recognizes the variants by name
///
/// This enum is the LSP / documentation / comptime SURFACE. `Proceed` and
/// `Return` are recognized by the compiler by variant NAME at a `before` hook
/// body's exit and lowered statically to a compiler-internal typed int tag +
/// branch over concrete locals (the D7-D native shape) — no `HookDecision` value
/// is ever built on the hot path, and this generic enum is NEVER routed through
/// the type resolver. As a consequence, do NOT hand-write a plain generic hook
/// signature `fn hook(...) -> HookDecision<Sig, State>` and expect the solver to
/// instantiate it; author the hook as a `before`-hook template
/// (`fn hook<Args>(args: Args) -> HookDecision<Args>`) and let the annotation
/// sugar install it — the sugar is the only entry.
///
/// ## First cut
///
/// - `Proceed(args)` continues to the target (or the next `before`) with the
///   argument pack `args` (today's compiler-internal mutation carrier;
///   `args[i]` resolves to the target's i-th parameter type). `return args` is
///   the documented shorthand for `return HookDecision::Proceed(args)`.
/// - `Return(result)` short-circuits: the target never runs, and `result` is
///   proved to be the target's declared return type `R` at the before-exit gate.
///   After-hooks still run on `result`.
/// - The no-State tuple form is the only one that ships; a State-threading
///   struct form is designed but not yet implemented (declare the hook without a
///   State parameter). See the E4 epic (#20).
///
/// ## Failure vocabulary
///
/// Only **propagate-typed-failure** ships: a hook returns a failure-valued `R`
/// via `return HookDecision::Return(<failure-valued result>)` (well-typed only
/// when `R` has a failure channel — `Result<T, E>` / `Option<T>`), and it
/// threads the after-chain as any success-valued `R` does. The `recover`,
/// `retry`, and `re-place` transforms are designed but not implemented in E4;
/// spelling one is a named surface-and-stop diagnostic (see issue #80).
pub enum HookDecision<Sig> {
    /// Continue to the target / next `before` with the argument pack.
    Proceed(Sig),
    /// Short-circuit with a result of the target's declared return type `R`.
    /// After-hooks still run on it.
    Return(Sig),
}
